Quasar-authored. Issue #418 — one GstCaps leaked per session.

gst_inter_pipe_sink_add_listener() takes a FULL ref on the listener's caps
(gst_inter_pipe_ilistener_get_caps() is transfer-full) and releases it on the
common tail (`if (srccaps) gst_caps_unref (srccaps);`). The
`if (!sinkcaps) goto add_to_list;` shortcut — taken whenever a listener attaches
before this sink has negotiated caps — jumps PAST that tail and leaks exactly one
GstCaps per attach.

Quasar always loses that race on the encode side: `build_encode_pipeline` sets
`caps` on `interpipesrc` as a construct property with `allow-renegotiation=false`,
so gst_inter_pipe_src_get_caps() reports negotiated and hands back the appsrc's
OWN private caps object (the copy gst_app_src_set_caps() made). The stray ref
therefore outlives the whole pipeline: after teardown the caps survives at
ref-count 1 with no live GstObject holding it — invisible to a GstObject-filtered
leaks tracer, and unbounded across sessions.

Reproduced standalone on Tower (two-pipeline interpipe topology, listener attach
before sink caps): 8 leaks / 8 cycles unpatched, 0 / 8 patched.

--- a/gst/interpipe/gstinterpipesink.c
+++ b/gst/interpipe/gstinterpipesink.c
@@ -980,8 +980,10 @@ gst_inter_pipe_sink_add_listener (GstInterPipeINode * iface,
   if (src_negotiated) {
     gboolean has_listeners;

-    if (!sinkcaps)
+    if (!sinkcaps) {
+      gst_caps_unref (srccaps);
       goto add_to_list;
+    }

     has_listeners = 0 != g_hash_table_size (listeners);

